<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Moravian Serbia</h1><p class="st-meta">1371 CE – 1402 CE · capital Kruševac</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Prince Lazar's Serbian state on the Morava, which led the resistance to the Ottomans at Kosovo in 1389 and founded a rich school of church art.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire</a> broke up, Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar Hrebeljanović</a>, a court official under Dušan, built the strongest of the successor states in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-morava">Morava</a> valley. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tvrtko-i-of-bosnia">Tvrtko</a> I he destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-altomanovic">Nikola Altomanović</a> in 1373, and by 1379 he was the leading Serbian ruler, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevac">Kruševac</a> as his capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a> ruled with nobles bound by family ties, including his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Vuk Branković</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novo-brdo">Novo Brdo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudnik">Rudnik</a> paid for the state, and Lazar reconciled the Serbian church with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1375. Churches such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravanica">Ravanica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazarica-church">Lazarica</a> gave their name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-morava">Morava</a> school of architecture.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a> died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> in 1389. His widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-milica-of-serbia">Milica</a> and son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-lazarevic">Stefan Lazarević</a> became Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. After fighting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> in 1402, Stefan received the title of despot from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>, and the state became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbian-despotate">Serbian Despotate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/moravian-serbia.jpg" alt="Moravian Serbia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Moravian_Serbia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1371 CE – 1402 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kruševac</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lazar, Milica, Stefan Lazarević</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Lazar, Lazar's Serbia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1386&amp;focus=moravian-serbia" aria-label="Moravian Serbia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5193 633 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Moravian Serbia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5513 889L5521 939M5513 889L5709 970M5513 889L5488 850M5513 889L5476 880"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5521 939h0M5709 970h0M5488 850h0M5476 880h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5513 889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5513 889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kruševac. <a href="/atlas?year=1386&amp;focus=moravian-serbia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
